+# AI log — SCRUM-105 (US7 · F-19 to F-23 transformer-layer state, backend) — 2026-10-30
+
+**Author:** Yeison Delgado · **Tool:** Claude (Claude Code)
+
+## What the AI generated
+- `backend/src/nodes/transformer-state.ts`: the RF-MAP-04/05 rule (`deriveTransformerState`), the phase
+  count from the transformer identifier, and `TRANSFORMER_SUMMARY_SQL`.
+- `transformer_state` in `GET /nodes` (`nodes.service.ts`, Swagger in `dto/nodes.dto.ts`).
+- Tests: `transformer-state.spec.ts`, new cases in `nodes.service.spec.ts`, e2e `test/transformer-state.e2e-spec.ts`.
+
+## What I did / reviewed
+- Took the rule from Scope v6.0: RF-MAP-04 (red = 2+ phases down, yellow = 1, gray = SMAC inactive, green
+  otherwise), RF-MAP-05 (count against the transformer's own phases) and §3.2 (a phase drop is a per-channel
+  event with activation and deactivation). The phase count comes from Technical Annex §3.3 (`node-T1-3`), and
+  the event codes from §4.2.
+- Reused the `PHASE_LOSS` pattern of the fault list (SCRUM-108), so both features read the same codes.
+- A phase is down when its last `05xx21y` event is SET; a CLEAR at the same instant wins. `05xx20y` (out of
+  range) does not count. Events are matched by the transformer `custom_id`, or by device when it is null.
+- Ran: 396 unit tests, lint, typecheck and build. The e2e test ran on PostgreSQL with Colbits' triggers: 11 new
+  tests, 28 together with the F-10, F-37 and F-29 suites. I loaded generator scenarios (1 phase down, 2 of 2,
+  3 phases, a recovered phase and a silent SMAC) and called the real `GET /nodes`. The results were WARNING,
+  CRITICAL, CRITICAL, NORMAL and INACTIVE, as expected. The frontend already reads `transformer_state`, and it
+  painted the transformer markers with no change.
